WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
Part 4. You will hear part of a lecture to medical students

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.360
about playing music to patients who are in hospital for surgery.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.400
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:11.919
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.959
Today's lecture is about studies that look at how music therapy can be used to help patients

00:01:20.959 --> 00:01:27.599
who undergo surgery. Now, most people undergo a surgical procedure at some point in their lives,

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.559
and more than 51 million operations are performed annually in the USA.

00:01:33.519 --> 00:01:38.400
But there's no escaping the fact that most patients feel uncomfortable following surgery.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.680
They may have difficulty mobilizing, and even sitting up in bed can feel too much.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.279
They may also be negatively affected by having their routine disturbed. You know,

00:01:49.279 --> 00:01:55.199
like if they can't do the things they normally do, or have to devote a lot of time to appointments,

00:01:55.199 --> 00:02:01.599
such as physiotherapy, to aid their recovery. Currently, the main strategy for improving

00:02:01.599 --> 00:02:07.919
recovery is medication to control pain. And this can be administered to patients in the short,

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:11.839
medium, or longer term, depending on the extent of their surgery.

00:02:12.559 --> 00:02:17.039
But music is still not an everyday part of the post-surgical phase,

00:02:17.039 --> 00:02:21.440
despite a wealth of relevant studies supporting its potential in recovery.

00:02:22.399 --> 00:02:27.600
Earlier this year, a research team set out to assess all the available evidence

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.360
so that they could highlight the potential for music in surgical recovery.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:39.279
They identified nearly 100 trials involving a total of 7,000 patients who were played recorded

00:02:39.279 --> 00:02:44.960
music as part of their post-operative care. The researchers then looked at what impact

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.720
the music had on the patients. They discovered that patients who had been played music

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.119
reported feeling happier and more satisfied in the post-operative phase,

00:02:55.679 --> 00:03:00.639
and the length of their stay was shorter than for patients who had not listened to any music.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.600
The researchers also explored the patient's choice of music, and their findings showed

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:14.960
that a wide variety of music styles was evident. However, a common factor was that the chosen music

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.919
had a calming quality. Some of the patients listened to music with headphones,

00:03:19.919 --> 00:03:24.880
but it was quiet enough not to prevent them from being able to communicate with nurses and other

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:33.360
staff. More often, though, the mode of delivery was by what are known as music pillows. These

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:39.360
broadcast sound that is only audible to the person lying on them. The research involved

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:46.639
testing music before, during, or after operations, or a combination. Some patients listened to the

00:03:46.639 --> 00:03:50.639
music just once a day, while others had several episodes a day.

00:03:55.919 --> 00:04:01.360
When asked to report their experience of listening to music, surgical patients said that either they

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:09.039
had no feelings of anxiety at any point, or those feelings were only slight. Patients who hadn't

00:04:09.039 --> 00:04:15.759
listened to music, on the other hand, reported higher levels of dissatisfaction. This feedback

00:04:15.759 --> 00:04:21.600
from all of the patients was then cross-checked against their medical notes, and in every case,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:28.160
those who were given music to listen to didn't need as much medication to ease their pain

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:34.799
as those patients who weren't played music. The type of music, patient choice and timing

00:04:34.799 --> 00:04:41.119
before, during, or after the surgery didn't make much difference, and it even worked when

00:04:41.119 --> 00:04:45.600
patients were played music under general anesthetic, although the positive effects

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.839
were greater when patients were awake. All the evidence suggests that music has a positive

00:04:51.839 --> 00:04:57.519
effect on post-operative patients, but it's not entirely clear how or why this is the case.

00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:03.760
A lot of people listen to music in daily life as a way to relax and forget their problems,

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.720
but the researchers came to the conclusion that it worked on patients by distraction.

00:05:09.679 --> 00:05:13.600
I suppose it was something familiar and gave them something they could control.

00:05:14.799 --> 00:05:19.920
The researchers say there is now sufficient research to demonstrate that music should be

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:26.480
available to all patients undergoing operations. They say patients should be able to choose what

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:33.279
they'd like to listen to, and if they prefer, recordings taken from nature can be just as good

00:05:33.279 --> 00:05:40.160
as music. Surgical teams may prefer patients to listen to music before the procedure or as soon

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:46.799
as they arrive back onto the ward. Clearly, there's more to learn about this area, and the

00:05:46.799 --> 00:05:53.119
team now plan to focus their next research on the most appropriate volume to play the music at,

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:59.040
and I look forward to reading the results of their study. It will be interesting to see that if it's

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:08.799
That is the end of part four. You now have one minute to check your answers to part four.

